ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".

MEMORY INSPECTION TOOLS (userspace)
----------------------------------

This xv6 tree includes several userspace utilities to inspect memory and
the kernel's `pageinfo` bookkeeping. Quick summary and notes about
behavior you may observe when using them:

- `memdump <addr> [len]` - dump memory at a virtual address; for kernel
	virtual addresses (>= KERNBASE) it uses `kread` to safely copy a page.
- `memdump_phys <addr> -p` - query the kernel `pageinfo` for a physical
	address and print the metadata (type, owner_pid, mapped_va, tag, ref).
- `dumppi` - scans the kernel's `pageinfo` array and prints all tracked
	(non-FREE) physical pages. This is authoritative *if* the kernel's
	pageinfo subsystem is populated.
- `dump_pages` / `dumpall` - trigger kernel dumps of the pageinfo table
	(and optionally page contents) to the kernel console. Useful for large
	batch exports.
- `procstat` + `get_pagemap(pid, buf, max)` - user-facing helpers that
	report per-process VA->PA mappings; useful as a fallback when the
	kernel's pageinfo table is empty or not fully populated.

Important behavior note (observed):

- In some runs `pageinfo_phys` / `dumppi` may report no tracked pages
	("No allocated/mapped pages found."). When that happens `memmap` will
	fall back to using `procstat` + `get_pagemap` to build a birds-eye
	map of physical pages from process page tables. The fallback is
	reliable for discovering USER pages but will not necessarily show
	kernel-owned pages (those are typically only visible via kernel
	bookkeeping like `pageinfo`). The tool also attempts to re-query
	`pageinfo_phys(pa)` for discovered PAs to refine the classification
	when possible.

How to verify:

- Run `dumppi` to see kernel-tracked pages. If it prints nothing,
	try `procstat` and `get_pagemap` (or `memmap`) to inspect process
	mappings.
- Use `memdump_phys <pa> -p` to check a single physical page's
	`pageinfo` details.

If you prefer, use `memmap -v` (if available) to dump raw discovered
entries (PA, type, owner_pid, mapped_va, tag, ref) for auditing and
verification.
